@app.cell(hide_code=True)
def _(mo, spatial_audit):
    mo.md(f"""
    ---

    ## Response to feedback

    **Place and confounding.** The feedback supplied for this revision asks how to separate
    student composition from location. We now compare areas and show unadjusted estimates
    beside models with state, population density and student enrollment controls. RQ1/RQ2
    also adjust for university density. These are adjusted associations, not causal effects;
    income, transport, resident composition and dealer coverage remain unmeasured.

    **Overlap.** The discussion also raised repeated use of listings in campus circles.
    We rebuild markets from listing records using mutually exclusive geographic assignments,
    pool student counts across universities, and compare county and CBSA results. The
    original 10-mile definition assigned {spatial_audit['campus_10mile_overlap']['multiple_match_percent']:.1f}%
    of matched listings to more than one university. Unique assignment removes this
    duplication but does not establish that neighboring markets are independent.

    **Question wording.** We replace university-level wording with area-level wording so
    the questions match the observations. The original and revised questions appear below.
    The changes respond to the unit of analysis and place concern, rather than selecting
    questions because their results are more significant.

    **Feedback record as of November 9.** The full Canvas comments have not been supplied.
    The Project Studio on November 11 has not yet occurred. This draft does not claim to
    record that meeting: the decisions discussed, the group's explanations and any resulting
    changes must be added after the meeting, together with any additional Canvas feedback.
    """)
    return


@app.cell(hide_code=True)
def _(mo):
    mo.md(r"""
    ---

    ## Research questions

    ### RQ1

    **Original:** Do universities with higher international student shares have different
    brand compositions in nearby used-car listings?

    **Revised:** Do areas with higher international student shares across their universities
    have different brand compositions in local used-car listings?

    **Columns:** `area_id`, `international_student_share`, `brand_*`, `n_known_brand`,
    `n_listings`, `population_density`, `college_density`, `total_students`, `state`.

    ### RQ2

    **Original:** Do universities with higher graduate student shares have different
    distributions of vehicle body styles and vehicle ages nearby?

    **Revised:** Do areas with higher graduate student shares across their universities have
    different distributions of body styles and vehicle ages in local used-car listings?

    **Columns:** `area_id`, `graduate_student_share`, `body_*`, `age_q10`, `age_q25`,
    `age_median`, `age_q75`, `age_q90`, `age_iqr`, `n_known_body`, `n_known_age`, and the
    same controls as RQ1.

    ### RQ3

    **Original:** Do areas with higher college density have a higher share of Japanese-brand
    vehicles in used-car listings? In M1 an area was a 10-mile campus circle.

    **Revised:** Do areas with higher university density have higher shares of Japanese-brand
    vehicles in local used-car listings?

    **Columns:** `area_id`, `n_colleges`, `land_sqmi`, `college_density`, `japanese_share`,
    `n_known_brand`, `population`, `population_density`, `total_students`, `state`.

    **Why all three changed:** Overlapping campus circles reuse the same market and do not
    isolate a university's student body. The new questions describe the geographic units
    we can observe. RQ3 retains its directional expectation, and examines whether it survives
    adjustment for population density.

    ### Common data and definitions

    - Universities are the M1-selected four-year, degree-granting IPEDS institutions, counted
      once per `UNITID`. An institution is not necessarily one physical campus.
    - The area international/graduate share is the sum of valid student counts divided by
      the corresponding sum of total enrollment, not an average of university percentages.
      The international measure uses the IPEDS nonresident-alien category as a proxy.
    - University density is institutions divided by **land** square miles. Population density
      is residents divided by the same land area. CBSA denominators include all member
      counties, including those without a university. These are different concepts.
    - CBSAs include metropolitan and micropolitan areas. Non-CBSA counties stay separate;
      they are not renamed CBSAs. A pure-CBSA sensitivity check is also reported.
    - We keep the contiguous 48 states and DC to compare identical geography. Brand,
      body-style and age analyses each use their own known-value denominator. Missing
      attributes do not remove a listing from unrelated analyses.
    - The primary known-listing threshold is 100. At least 90% of selected universities in
      an area must have positive known enrollment, and each student-share denominator must
      cover at least 90% of known enrollment. We compare thresholds of 50 and 200 later.

    ### Reproducible loading and geographic merge

    The cells below repeat the two M1 cleaning cells, then merge listings and universities
    with county/CBSA geography and population. Original inputs come from a fixed team-repository
    commit, or identical local repository files; SHA256 checks reject changed inputs.

    The only precomputed step is a coordinate-to-county lookup made from the full 2019
    TIGER/Line polygons. It is embedded with its input hashes so this notebook remains a
    single portable file. `scripts/prepare_m2_spatial.py` reproduces that lookup; no model
    results are embedded. Boundary points use the first GEOID in sorted order, without
    snapping unmatched points. All grouping, plots and statistical results are computed here.

    Additional sources are [2019 county boundaries](https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/TIGER2019/COUNTY/tl_2019_us_county.zip),
    [September 2018 CBSA membership](https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/metro-micro/geographies/reference-files/2018/delineation-files/list1_Sep_2018.xls),
    and [2019 county population estimates](https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2010-2019/counties/totals/co-est2019-alldata.csv).
    Population is a 2019 resident estimate, not an ACS five-year estimate. No GDP or income
    data are used in this version.
    """)
    return


